{"visualPremise":"A flat 2D stickman-doodle explainer that tracks one visual subject — the cultural image of the 'horned Viking' — across a few distinct, repeatable locations (battle hillside, Bronze Age ritual site, excavation trench, 19th‑century theater, museum display).","visualTone":"informative with wry distance: clear and slightly ironic (we're debunking a dramatic image), balancing visceral spectacle (the imagined horned charge) with quiet, evidence-first museum and archaeology scenes.","cast":[{"id":"hero","role":"the horned-Viking icon — the single wrong picture that circulates across time"},{"id":"viewer","role":"the viewer's second-person avatar used in the opening charge scene"},{"id":"archaeologist","role":"a generic archaeologist who inspects finds and points out absence/presence of evidence"},{"id":"19th_century_costume_designer","role":"the Romantic/operatic costume designer figure who first popularizes the horned look onstage"},{"id":"bronze_age_priest","role":"the wearer of ritual/ceremonial horned headgear from the Nordic Bronze Age context"},{"id":"plain_viking_warrior","role":"the evidence-grounded Viking-age warrior as reconstructed from archaeology (no horns)"}],"settings":[{"id":"setting_imagined_battlefield_hillside_dramatic_s","label":"imagined battlefield hillside (dramatic, stylized)"},{"id":"setting_bronze_age_ritual_bog_find_location_open","label":"Bronze Age ritual/ bog find location (open field with peat bog marker)"},{"id":"setting_archaeological_excavation_trench_grid_la","label":"archaeological excavation trench (grid, labeled finds, trowel and brush)"},{"id":"setting_19th_century_theater_stage_atelier_stage","label":"19th-century theater stage/atelier (stage backdrop, costume racks)"},{"id":"setting_museum_display_case_pedestal_label_plaqu","label":"museum display case (pedestal, label plaque, glass casing)"}],"props":[{"id":"gjermundbu_helmet","label":"The Gjermundbu-style helmet: a simple rounded iron dome with an integrated 'spectacle' guard curving over the eye region and a short nose guard; no horn attachments, no rivet patterns for horns. Dull iron-gray fill, thin black outline. Display on a small museum pedestal with a label reading 'Gjermundbu helmet, c. 970'."},{"id":"veksø_bronze_helm","label":"Veksø-type bronze ceremonial helmet: thin decorative bronze cap with long gently curved horns ending in small round discs; horns are narrower and shorter than the icon horns and look fragile. Warm copper fill, horns off-white tips. Often shown with a watery/bog or ritual context."},{"id":"theater_prop_helmet","label":"Theatrical horned helmet prop: bold, exaggerated horns (same silhouette as the horned-Viking icon) but visually heavier and stylized for stage: thicker horn cross-section and larger scale. Colored with slightly brighter pigments to read at a distance (burgundy accent available)."},{"id":"museum_label","label":"Small rectangular placard: pale gray rectangle with two lines of text (title line bold, secondary line small). Use once next to actual archaeological pieces (e.g., 'Gjermundbu helmet, c. 970') to visually cue 'authentic museum object'."}],"graphicLanguage":{"comparisonLayouts":"Side-by-side comparisons always align helmets at the same baseline for easy silhouette reading. Use captions in cool blue for archaeological items and sepia for theatrical/cultural items.","timelineConventions":"Horizontal timeline bar with three labeled nodes: 'Bronze Age (c. 900–1100 BCE)' — copper node; 'Viking Age (c. 793–1066 CE)' — iron-gray node; '19th century Romantic/theatre' — sepia node. Connect the horned icon with arrows showing cultural borrowing.","symbolicMetaphorLanguage":"Use the horned-Viking icon as a recurring metaphor: when the narration says 'this image stuck', show the icon stamped repeatedly like a simple rubber-stamp motif fading into postcards, theater posters, and a modern football helmet."},"factualVisualConstraints":["Do not invent physical horn attachment marks on genuine Viking-age helmet reconstructions (e.g., Gjermundbu).","Veksø bronze helmets must be clearly labeled Bronze Age and look decorative, not battle-ready.","Theatrical horned helmets must be shown as 19th-century costume props, visually distinct from Bronze Age originals."]}

CALLBACK PLAN: planted detail "two long curved horns" — plant in segment seg1, payoff in segment seg14.

